$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: General Procedure for the Baylis-Hillman
reaction: Enantioselective Baylis-Hillman Reaction Employing 10. To a 10
mL round bottom flask, flame dried and equipped with a stir bar, was
added peptide 10 (0.0015 mmol), dissolved in CHCl3/THF (1:2, 0.5M),
L-proline (0.0015 mmol), aldehyde 1 (0.015 mmol), and methyl vinyl ketone
(0.029 mmol). The resulting mixture was stirred until complete loss of
starting material was observed by TLC (2:1 hexanes/ethyl acetate). The
reaction was diluted with chloroform and purification via flash
chromatography (SiO2, CHCl3). 2.7 mg (81%) of 2 as a pale yellow oil.
3-[Hydroxy-(4-nitro-phenyl)-methyl]-but-3-en-2-one (22). Characterization
of compound 22 has been previously reported.3 Proof of absolute
stereochemistry. The absolute stereochemistry of the major adduct 22 was
determined by comparing the sign of the specific rotation with reported
literature data2 using the partial kinetic resolution of the racemic
compound 22 by Sharpless epoxidation using L-(+)-diethyl tartrate, which
should react preferentially with compound (S)-22. Measurement of the
optical rotation and comparison to the literature showed the sample to be
of the (R)-configuration. (R)-configuration: [α]D -3.8 (0.53, CHCl3);
Literature for (3R)-3-[Hydroxy-(4-nitro-phenyl)-methyl]-but-3-en-2-one:
[α]D -12.1 (0.53, CHCl3)
