$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM A 100 mL round-bottom flask was charged with (R)-BINOL (535 mg, 1.87
mmol, 0.1 equiv) and 17 mL of methylene chloride. To this solution was
added powdered 4 angstrom molecular sieves (7.5 g, kept in 120 °C oven
for 2 days) followed by Ti(O-iPr)4 (0.56 mL, 1.87mmol, 0.1 equiv) and the
resultant dark red mixture was refluxed for 1 h and then allowed to cool
to room temperature. A solution of known trifluoromethanesulfonic acid
4-formylphenyl ester1 (4.74 g, 18.7 mmol, 1 equiv) in 3.6 mL of methylene
chloride was added to the mixture and stirred for 10 min. The solution
was cooled to -78 °C, and allyltributyltin (6.99 mL, 22.4 mmol, 1.2
equiv) was added as a steady stream over 2 min. The flask was sealed with
an unpunctured septa and placed in a -20 °C freezer without stirring for
84 h. The reaction was quenched with 50 mL of saturated aqueous NaHCO3.
The mixture was diluted with 50 mL of methylene chloride, allowed to warm
to room temperature and stirred for 2 h. Following filtration through
Celite, the filter cake was washed thoroughly with 3 x 30 mL methylene
chloride, and the combined filtrate was dried over Na2SO4, filtered, and
concentrated in vacuo. The crude residue was purified by flash
chromatography (10 to 50% dichloromethane/petroleum ether). Alcohol,
yield 4.85 g, 87%, 95% ee. Yellow oil. [α]25D = +41.5 (c 0.2, CH2Cl2); IR
(neat/NaCl) 3391, 2920, 1429 cm-1; 1H NMR (500 MHz, CDCl3) δ 7.47 (d, J =
8.8, 2 H), 7.27 (d, J = 8.8, 2 H), 5.74-5.85 (m, 1 H), 5.21 (s, 1 H),
5.18 (d, J = 5.5, 1 H), 4.81-4.78 (m, 1H), 2.42-2.57 (m, 2 H), 2.13 (d, J
= 2.8, 1 H); 13C NMR (125 MHz, CDCl3) δ 148.7, 144.3, 133.6, 127.6,
121.3, 120.0, 119.3, 72.2, 43.9; HRMS (ELS) m/z calcd for C11H11F3NaO4S
[M +Na]+ 319.0228, found 319.0222; Retention time of the major R-isomer
was 25.91 min, and the minor S-isomer had a retention time of 27.98 min.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM stereoselective, catalyst from stage 1 added to stage 3, molecular sieves
used
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Synthesis and Structural Reassignment of (+)-Epicalyxin F
