$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM TABLE 1, ENTRY 11:
(4R,7R,Z)-2-Bromo-7-((tert-butyldiphenylsilyloxy)methyl)-5-methylnona-1,5-dien-4-ol
(26): The (R,R)-1,2-diamino-1,2-diphenylethane-bis-N,Ntoluenesulfonamide
(288 mg, 0.55 mmol) was dried under high vacuum at 100 °C overnight, then
cooled to rt and dissolved in CH2Cl2 (5.0 mL). The resulting solution was
cooled to 0 °C and boron tribromide (0.55 mL, 1.0 M solution in CH2Cl2)
was added. The mixture was stirred at 0 °C for an additional 10 min and
then allowed to warm to rt with stirring for 1 h. The solvent was then
removed under high vacuum to give a pale yellow solid which was
redissolved in CH2Cl2 (4 mL). After stirring for 10 min, solvent was
again removed with stirring under high vacuum. The residue was
redissolved in dry CH2Cl2 (2.0 mL) and cooled to 0 °C. Then the freshly
prepared 2-bromo-3-(tri-n-butylstannyl)-1-propene (225 mg, 0.55 mmol) in
CH2Cl2 (1.5 mL) was added. The resulting mixture was warmed to rt and
stirred for an additional 12 h. The gold solution was then cooled to -78
°C and a solution of aldehyde 14 (155 mg, 0.41 mmol) in CH2Cl2 (2.0 mL)
was introduced via syringe. The resulting mixture was stirred at -78 °C
for an additional 2 h. The reaction was quenched by addition of pH 7
buffer (2.5 mL). The mixture was warmed to rt and poured into a solution
of potassium fluoride (5.0 mL, 2.0 N aq). The resulting mixture was
stirred at rt for 30 min and two layers were separated. The organic layer
was collected and the aqueous phase was extracted with Et2O (30 mL x 3).
The combined organic layers were washed with pH 7 buffer (30 mL x 2) and
brine (100 mL) respectively. The solution was dried over sodium sulphate
and concentrated in vacuo. The crude product was purified with flash
silica gel column chromatography (hexanes:EtOAc = 6:1) to provide the
allylation product 26. Analytical HPLC on a silica (2) column using 10%
EtOAc in hexanes showed the product to be at least 99:1 dr. Colorless oil
175 mg, 0.35 mmol, 85% (99:1). Rf = 0.30 (hexanes:EtOAc = 5:1); [α]D22
-57.7 (c 1.02, CHCl3); IR (thin film, cm-1): 3454, 3071, 2958, 2929,
2857, 1689, 1642, 1462, 1425, 1206; 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ (ppm): 7.69
- 7.66 (m, 4H), 7.44 - 7.37 (m, 6H), 5.68 (s, 1H), 5.51 (d, J = 1.5 Hz,
1H), 5.01 (d, J = 9.7 Hz, 1H), 4.83 (ddd, J = 7.7, 4.8, 2.7 Hz, 1H), 3.59
(dd, J = 9.8, 5.4 Hz, 1H), 3.42 (dd, J = 9.8, 7.3 Hz, 1H), 2.74 (dd, J =
14.6, 8.3 Hz, 1H), 2.64 - 2.57 (m, 1H), 2.52 (dd, J = 14.6, 4.9 Hz, 1H),
1.75 (d, J = 0.9 Hz, 3H), 1.65 (d, J = 2.4 Hz, 1H), 1.54 - 1.48 (m, 1H),
1.18 - 1.09 (m, 1H), 1.06 (s, 9H),0.83 (t, J = 7.6 Hz, 3H); 13C NMR (100
MHz, CDCl3) δ (ppm): 137.3, 135.63, 135.61, 133.7, 133.6, 131.4, 130.7,
129.7, 127.68, 127.66, 119.0, 67.6, 67.3, 46.6, 41.9, 26.9, 24.6, 19.3,
17.8, 11.8; HRMS-ESI (calc. for C27H37O2SiBrNa [M+Na]+) 523.1644, found
523.1634.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM stereoselective, agent dependent stereochemistry, in-situ generated
reactant in stage 2, buffered solution used in stage 4
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Stereocontrol in Asymmetric SE' Reactions of γ-Substituted
α,β-Unsaturated Aldehydes
