$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM To a stirred solution of methyl 4-formylbenzoate (500 mg, 3.05 mmol),
aqueous trimethylamine (24% wt, 0.94 mL, 3.89 mmol) in methanol (3 mL)
was added methyl acrylate (790 mg, 9.18 mmol). The resulting clear
mixture was stirred at room temperature for 20 h (monitored by TLC). Then
chloroform (20 mL) and water (5 mL) were added and the mixture was
stirred for another 2 min. The organic phase was separated and the water
phase was extracted with chloroform (2 x 10 mL). The combined organic
phase was dried over anhydrous magnesium sulfate. After removing of the
solvent and low bolting-point substances the crude product was purified
by column chromatography (25% ethyl acetate in hexanes) to afford the
pure adduct S2. The 1H NMR data for this compound matched in all respect
with that reported in the literature.[5] White solid, yield 648 mg, 86%.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Baylis-Hillman reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Enantioselective Intermolecular [2 + 2 + 2] Cycloadditions of Ene-Allenes
with Allenoates
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Brusoe, Andrew T.; Edwankar, Rahul V.; Alexanian, Erik J.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2012), 14(23), 6096-6099

